1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,354
Every event contract gives you a price. It
does not tell you who could know first.

2
00:00:05,554 --> 00:00:08,911
LevelField is a pre-trade risk layer for
DreamDEX.

3
00:00:08,911 --> 00:00:11,901
It measures structural information
asymmetry

4
00:00:11,901 --> 00:00:14,484
before a trader or agent takes a side.

5
00:00:14,684 --> 00:00:18,936
Start with a captured DreamDEX price
binary on Somnia Shannon.

6
00:00:18,936 --> 00:00:22,776
LevelField scores it three out of one
hundred: low risk.

7
00:00:22,976 --> 00:00:26,694
No participant controls the global
reference price.

8
00:00:26,694 --> 00:00:31,140
Its outcome is public, and disclosure is
nearly simultaneous.

9
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:33,109
Now change only the event.

10
00:00:33,109 --> 00:00:38,145
This curated reference contract resolves
on one person's private decision.

11
00:00:38,345 --> 00:00:42,639
The same engine returns ninety-five out of
one hundred: high risk.

12
00:00:42,839 --> 00:00:45,017
One person controls the outcome.

13
00:00:45,017 --> 00:00:48,829
No clear restriction stops an early knower
from trading.

14
00:00:48,829 --> 00:00:51,723
Circuit breaker one sets a high-risk
floor.

15
00:00:51,923 --> 00:00:55,379
That three-to-ninety-five contrast is the
product.

16
00:00:55,379 --> 00:00:59,802
LevelField explains who can know first,
not which side will win.

17
00:01:00,002 --> 00:01:03,917
It does not allege wrongdoing or detect
live insider activity.

18
00:01:04,117 --> 00:01:08,341
Under the interface is a public,
five-dimension anchor library.

19
00:01:08,341 --> 00:01:12,095
A model classifies each dimension against
those anchors.

20
00:01:12,295 --> 00:01:16,247
Every evidence quote must match the
contract's exact words.

21
00:01:16,247 --> 00:01:20,065
Code rejects instruction-like text aimed
at the assessor.

22
00:01:20,265 --> 00:01:22,022
If information is missing,

23
00:01:22,022 --> 00:01:26,954
LevelField defaults conservatively to
level four instead of guessing low.

24
00:01:27,154 --> 00:01:30,378
Then deterministic code applies fixed
weights,

25
00:01:30,378 --> 00:01:33,461
cross-dimension rules, and circuit
breakers.

26
00:01:33,461 --> 00:01:36,510
The model never generates the numeric
score.

27
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:37,068
Here,

28
00:01:37,068 --> 00:01:40,321
an agent calls the real LevelField MCP
server

29
00:01:40,321 --> 00:01:43,646
over standard input and output before it
acts.

30
00:01:43,846 --> 00:01:47,753
The policy is visible: low or moderate
risk can proceed.

31
00:01:47,753 --> 00:01:50,125
Elevated or high risk is declined.

32
00:01:50,325 --> 00:01:53,444
The DreamDEX example returns PROCEED at
three.

33
00:01:53,444 --> 00:01:57,560
The individual-decision case returns
DECLINE at ninety-five,

34
00:01:57,560 --> 00:01:59,275
with the reason attached.

35
00:01:59,475 --> 00:02:05,344
After a provenance-complete republish,
each current score will bind its band,

36
00:02:05,344 --> 00:02:11,441
five dimensions, method hash, timestamp,
and immutable source on Somnia Shannon.

37
00:02:11,641 --> 00:02:14,149
The verifier reads every field back and

38
00:02:14,149 --> 00:02:16,978
fails closed on anything missing or
changed.

39
00:02:17,178 --> 00:02:21,997
Across sixteen curated contracts, scores
span three to ninety-five.

40
00:02:21,997 --> 00:02:27,966
Category risk rises in the expected order,
with a Spearman rho of point nine three.

41
00:02:28,166 --> 00:02:32,603
Sixty-nine software tests and eight
smart-contract tests pass.

42
00:02:32,603 --> 00:02:38,163
The official DreamDEX SDK independently
cross-checks active-market discovery,

43
00:02:38,163 --> 00:02:40,762
read-only and without a private key.

44
00:02:40,962 --> 00:02:46,028
We delivered eleven evidence-backed SDK
and documentation findings.

45
00:02:46,028 --> 00:02:49,925
As DreamDEX grows, LevelField helps
agents, venues,

46
00:02:49,925 --> 00:02:53,363
and traders know who can know before they
do.
